                  <msContents>
                     <msItem n="1">
                        <locus>ff. 1v-8v</locus>
                        <title ref="n2y_zty_43c" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_274">Milot
                           ha-Higayon (Treatise on Logical Terminology)</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_274">מלות ההגיון</title>
                        <author ref="uxd_pmr_m3c" key="person_100185495">
                           <persName>Maimonides, Moses, 1135-1204</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">משה בן מימון</persName>
                        </author>
                        <respStmt>
                           <resp>Hebrew translation by </resp>
                           <persName key="person_64172358">Moses ben Samuel ibn Tibbon</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">משה בן שמואל יבן תבון</persName>
                        </respStmt>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="2">
                        <locus>ff. 11r-21r</locus>
                        <title ref="zxf_pmr_m3c" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3359">Mesharet
                           Mosheh</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3359">משרת משה</title>
                        <author key="person_7067">
                           <persName>Qalonymos ben Qalonymos ben Meʾir, 1286-</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">קלונימוס בן קלונימוס</persName>
                        </author>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="3">
                        <locus>ff. 21v-30v</locus>
                        <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3360">Ruaḥ Ḥen</title>
                        <title ref="o4g_pmr_m3c" xml:lang="he" key="work_3360">רוח חן</title>
                        <author>Anonymous</author>
                        <note>An introductory work on medieval science and philosophy, which draws
                           heavily on the works of <persName key="person_100185495"
                              >Maimonides</persName>, especially the <hi rend="italic">Guide for the
                              Perplexed</hi>.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                  </msContents>
                  <physDesc>
                     <handDesc>
                        <summary>Ashkenazic semi-cursive scripts</summary>
                        <handNote>No. 1 was copied by the scribe <persName>Yosef b. Israel <foreign
                                 xml:lang="he">יוסף בן ישראל</foreign></persName>, who left a
                           colophon on <locus>f. 8v</locus>; Nos. 2 and 3 were copied by two
                           anonymous scribes who changed hands at the end of a quire, three folios
                           after the beginning of No. 3 (<locus>f. 22v</locus>). Each of these
                           scribes used different paper. The second and third scribes, who evidently
                           collaborated closely and were contemporaries of <persName>Yosef b.
                              Israel</persName> judging from their scripts, did not leave
                           colophons.</handNote>
                     </handDesc>
                     <decoDesc>
                        <p> In No. 1 (<locus>ff. 1v-8v</locus>) and the final folio of No. 2
                              (<locus>f. 21r</locus>), some simple, monochrome penwork decoration
                           around initial words and text blocks. </p>
                     </decoDesc>
                  </physDesc>
                  <history>
                     <origin><origPlace>[Italy?]</origPlace>
                        <origDate calendar="#Gregorian" when="1463">[1463 (No. 1) and c. 1463 (Nos.
                           2-3]</origDate>
                     </origin>
                  </history>
               </msPart>
               <msPart>
                  <msIdentifier>
                     <altIdentifier>
                        <idno type="part">Part II (Nos. 4-9)</idno>
                     </altIdentifier>
                  </msIdentifier>
                  <msContents>
                     <msItem n="4">
                        <locus>ff. 39v-102v</locus>
                        <title ref="v52_pmr_m3c" key="work_3361">The Aphorisms of
                           Hippocrates</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3361">אמפוריזמי לאפוקרט</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3361">Amforizmi le-Apoḳraṭ</title>
                        <author ref="uxc_pmr_m3c" key="person_287984736">
                           <persName>Hippocrates</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">אפוקרט</persName>
                        </author>
                        <respStmt>
                           <resp>Hebrew translation by </resp>
                           <persName key="person_64172358">Moses ben Samuel ibn Tibbon</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">משה בן שמואל יבן תבון</persName>
                        </respStmt>
                        <note>There are two parallel Hebrew translations of the <hi rend="italic"
                              >Aphorisms</hi>, one of which is copied on the margins.</note>
                        <note>The translations are accompanied by a Hebrew translation of <persName
                              key="person_100185495">Maimonides'</persName> commentary on the <hi
                              rend="italic">Aphorisms</hi> by <persName key="person_64172358">Moses
                              ibn Tibbon</persName>.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="5">
                        <locus>f. 103v</locus>
                        <title ref="jfy_zty_43c" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3364">Maʾamar
                           Rambam</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3364">מאמר רמב״ם</title>
                        <title key="work_3364">The Decree of Rambam</title>
                        <author ref="uxd_pmr_m3c" key="person_100185495">
                           <persName>Maimonides, Moses, 1135-1204</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">משה בן מימון</persName>
                        </author>
                        <note>An astronomical and astrological fragment, adapted from extracts from
                           Maimonides' letter to the scholars of Lunel and Montpellier.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="6">
                        <locus>ff. 104r-110v</locus>
                        <title ref="tjw_zty_43c" key="work_3362">An Abridgement of the <hi
                              rend="italic">Sefer Yesod ʿOlam</hi></title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3362">קצור ספר יסוד עולם</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3362">Ḳitsur Sefer Yesod
                           ʿOlam</title>
                        <author ref="a1d_pmr_m3c" key="person_21765128">
                           <persName>Yitsḥaḳ ben Yosef, active 13th-14th century</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">יצחק בן יוסף הישראלי</persName>
                        </author>
                        <note> The abridgement in Arabic was made by <persName key="person_7068"
                              >Yosef ben Yitsḥaḳ Israeli</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">יוסף בן יצחק הישראלי</persName>, the son of the
                           author.</note>
                        <note>The Hebrew translation of the abridgement was possibly by another
                           member of the same family, <persName key="person_7058">Yitsḥaḳ ben
                              Shelomoh ben Yitsḥak Israeli</persName>.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="7">
                        <locus>f. 111r-v</locus>
                        <title key="work_3363">Judgement of the Seven Climates</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3363">משפט שבעה האקלמים</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3363">Mishpaṭ Shivʿah
                           ha-Aḳlimim</title>
                        <author>Anonymous</author>
                        <note>A short and independent treatise on the seven climates, found in many
                           manuscripts.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="8">
                        <locus>ff. 112r-118v</locus>
                        <title ref="x3y_zty_43c" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_252">ʿOlam
                           Kaṭan</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_252">עולם קטן</title>
                        <author ref="xyd_pmr_m3c" key="person_64172358">
                           <persName>Moses ben Samuel ibn Tibbon</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">משה בן שמואל יבן תבון</persName>
                        </author>
                        <note>A philosophical treatise on the immortality of the soul. The
                           authorship of Moses ibn Tibbon is uncertain; the work has also been
                           attributed to his father, <persName key="person_97166726">Samuel ibn
                              Tibbon</persName>.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem n="9">
                        <locus>ff. 119r-130v</locus>
                        <title ref="ijg_pmr_m3c" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc" key="work_3365">Tsintsenet
                           ha-Man</title>
                        <title xml:lang="he" key="work_3365">צנצנת המן</title>
                        <author ref="uxd_pmr_m3c" key="person_100185495">
                           <persName>Maimonides, Moses, 1135-1204</persName>
                           <persName xml:lang="he">משה בן מימון</persName>
                        </author>
                        <note>The prefaces to the Hebrew translation of <persName
                              key="person_100185495">Maimonides'</persName> commentary on the
                           Mishnah.</note>
                        <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                     </msItem>
                  </msContents>
                  <physDesc>
                     <handDesc>
                        <summary>Italian semi-cursive scripts</summary>
                        <handNote><p>No. 4-6 were copied by <persName>Mordekhai b. Binyamin <foreign
                                    xml:lang="he">מרדכי בן בנימין</foreign></persName>, who left his
                              colophon on <locus>f. 102r</locus>.</p>
                           <p>Nos. 7-9 were copied by a different scribe, who name may have been
                                 <persName ref="m3n_smr_m3c">Moses <foreign xml:lang="he"
                                    >משה</foreign></persName>.</p>
                        </handNote>
                     </handDesc>
                     <decoDesc>
                        <p>At the beginning of No. 4 (<locus>f. 39r</locus>), two figural drawings
                           (of a dragon and a stork respectively) in black ink, possibly by the
                           scribe.</p>
                        <p> In No. 5-9 (<locus>ff. 103v-130v</locus>), simple penwork drawings and flourishes
                           around initial words in red and black ink. </p>
                     </decoDesc>
                  </physDesc>
                  <history>
                     <origin>
                        <origPlace>[Italy?]</origPlace>
                        <origDate calendar="#Gregorian" when="1375">[c. 1375]</origDate>
                     </origin>
                  </history>
               </msPart>
               <msPart>
                  <msIdentifier>
                     <altIdentifier>
                        <idno type="part">Part III (No. 10)</idno>
                     </altIdentifier>
                  </msIdentifier>
                  <msContents>
                     <msItem n="10">
                        <locus>f. 131r-v</locus>
                        <title>Judgements of Constellations</title>
                        <title ref="jqm_smr_m3c" xml:lang="he">משפטי המזלות</title>
                        <title ref="hpg_pmr_m3c" xml:lang="he-Latn-x-lc">Mishpeṭei
                           ha-Mazalot</title>
                        <author>Anonymous</author>
                        <note>A fragment of a treatise on medical astrology.</note>
                     </msItem>
                  </msContents>
                  <physDesc>
                     <handDesc>
                        <handNote>Italian cursive script</handNote>
                     </handDesc>
                  </physDesc>
                  <history>
                     <origin><origPlace>[Italy</origPlace>
                        <origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1550" notAfter="1600"> late 16th
                           cent.?]</origDate></origin>
                  </history>
               </msPart>
